    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the requirement.\n\n**How should error recovery preserve user work?** should preserve local intent and user work first, then synchronize or retry without silently overwriting newer state.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the source of truth, boundary, success path, failure path, and ownership. Choose the smallest design that satisfies the requirement before introducing additional infrastructure.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core algorithm, state transition, lookup, or policy so it is easy to explain in a senior interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Item {\n  id: string;\n  enabled: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction activeItems(\n  items: readonly Item[],\n): Item[] {\n  const result: Item[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i += 1) {\n    if (items[i].enabled) {\n      result.push(items[i]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst active = activeItems([\n  { id: \"one\", enabled: true },\n  { id: \"two\", enabled: false },\n]);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform APIs when they improve readability without hiding an important architectural trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst items = [\n  { id: \"one\", enabled: true },\n  { id: \"two\", enabled: false },\n];\n\nconst active = items.filter(\n  (item) => item.enabled,\n);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **How should error recovery preserve user work?** with an explicit owner, failure path, measurement strategy, and migration plan.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and scale:\nExplain the dominant cost and how it changes with growth, retries, users, data, events, or deployments. Then describe what you would cache, batch, partition, isolate, or measure.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty state, partial failure, stale data, retries, cancellation, unauthorized access, slow network, rollout failure, and recovery where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the boundary, source of truth, failure/recovery path, observability signal, and key trade-off.",
